    "Theoretical models explain item preference distributions and KL divergence in Semantic ID (SID) prefix trees.",
    "These models are articulated through two key theorems in the paper 'REGER: Modeling inter-item Relations with Semantic IDs for Generative Recommendation' (arXiv:2610.37584v1).",
    "Theorem 4.1 is called Exact Preference Factorization.",
    "Theorem 4.1 establishes that the preference probability of any item can be exactly factorized based on its SID.",
    "Item preferences are expressed as conditional distributions over legal next tokens according to Theorem 4.1.",
    "This factorization allows for a structured understanding of how user preferences can be decomposed hierarchically along the SID prefix tree.",
    "Theorem 4.2 is called Hierarchical KL Decomposition.",
    "Theorem 4.2 provides a method to decompose the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence between two item preference distributions, P and Q, over the same interaction history.",
    "The item-level KL divergence can be represented as a weighted sum of conditional KL divergences across all nonterminal prefixes in the SID prefix tree according to Theorem 4.2.",
    "Each weight in the decomposition corresponds to the prefix probability mass under distribution P.",
    "This decomposition connects item-level preferences to the conditional distributions of next tokens.",
    "This facilitates a deeper understanding of user preferences in a hierarchical context.",
    "These theoretical frameworks clarify the relationships between item preferences.",
    "These frameworks motivate the design of the Hierarchical Collaborative Preference Distillation (HCPD) method.",
    "The HCPD method leverages these decompositions for effective recommendation training."
] 

